/-! # A flat-proper refinement of a finitely presented subtrusive morphism

Source: Rydh, Submersions and effective descent of étale morphisms, Theorem (3.10),
affine-base case, printed p.14.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-subtrusive-fp-flat-proper-refinement)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Over an affine base, a universally subtrusive finitely presented scheme morphism has a
refinement factoring through a faithfully flat finitely presented map and a proper surjective
finitely presented map. Finite presentation is expressed as local finite presentation,
quasi-compactness and quasi-separatedness. There is no flatness assumption on `f` and no
Noetherian assumption on the base.
Source: Rydh, Theorem (3.10), affine-base case, printed p.14.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-subtrusive-fp-flat-proper-refinement)

Proof plan from the accepted DAG split:

1. Choose $p:C\to Y$ from [lem-sfb-affine-integral-compact-generics-cover]. Put $Z=X\times_Y C$.
By [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence], $h:Z\to C$ is finitely presented and $Z$ is qcqs. By
[lem-sfb-subtrusive-topological-descent-calculus], $h$ is universally subtrusive. All schemes
here are quasi-separated, as required by that calculus: $C$ and $Y$ are affine and the maps to
them are finitely presented.

2. Apply [lem-sfb-compact-generics-flat-open] to $h$: the cover provides reducedness and
quasi-compactness of $C_{\mathrm{gen}}$. Obtain a dense quasi-compact open $U\subseteq C$
containing every generic point of an irreducible component of $C$, over which $h$ is flat. Its
restriction is locally of finite presentation by base change in
[lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence]. Apply [lem-trf-flattening-by-admissible-blowup] to
$Z\to C$ and $U$. Finite presentation gives the required finite type and quasi-separatedness.
This gives a $U$-admissible blowup $q:B\to C$ and a closed strict transform $W\subseteq
Z\times_C B$ such that $a:W\to B$ is flat and of finite presentation.

3. The centre ideal is finite type and equal to the unit ideal on $U$ by
[def-nagata-admissible-blowup]. Thus [thm-nagata-global-blowup-proper] makes $q$ proper, and
[thm-nagata-global-blowup-complement] identifies its inverse image of $U$ with $U$. Its closed
image contains dense $U$, so $q$ is surjective. By
[lemma-nagata-conrad-dense-in-admissible-blowup], the same $U$ is dense in $B$. We have only
asserted that $q$ is proper, not that it is finitely presented.

4. Let $\mathcal I$ be the centre of $q$ and $E=V(\mathcal I\mathcal O_B)=q^{-1}(V(\mathcal
I))$; since $\mathcal I|_U=\mathcal O_U$, $U\subseteq C\setminus V(\mathcal I)$. Every generic
point $\xi$ of an irreducible component of $B$ lies in $q^{-1}(U)$: by
[thm-nagata-blowup-principalizes-center], near $\xi$ the ideal $\mathcal I\mathcal O_B$ is
generated by a non-zero-divisor $t$, which stays a non-zero-divisor in the localization
$\mathcal O_{B,\xi}$; that local ring has a single prime, so a non-unit $t$ would be nilpotent,
impossible; hence $t$ is a unit and $\xi\notin E$. By [thm-nagata-global-blowup-complement],
$B\setminus E\to C\setminus V(\mathcal I)$ is an isomorphism of open subschemes, so $q(\xi)$ is
a generic point of an irreducible component of the open $C\setminus V(\mathcal I)$, hence of
$C$, and $q(\xi)\in U$ by step 2. (Topological density of $U$ in $B$ alone would not give this
when $B$ has infinitely many components.) Now let $b\in B$, choose a generic point $\xi$ of an
irreducible component containing $b$, and an affine open $T\subseteq B$ containing $b$, hence
$\xi$. The base change of $f$ along the affine $T\to Y$ is $(Z\times_CB)\times_BT\to T$, so by
the definition of universal subtrusiveness in the statement (equivalently by base change in
[lem-sfb-subtrusive-topological-descent-calculus]) the pair $b\in\overline{\{\xi\}}$ lifts to
$z\in\overline{\{z_\xi\}}$ in $Z\times_CB$. The strict transform $W$ is the scheme-theoretic
closure of the open subscheme of $Z\times_CB$ over $B\setminus E$; the immersion of that open
factors through its scheme-theoretic image [def-nagata-scheme-theoretic-image], so $W$ is closed
and contains the inverse image of $q^{-1}(U)$. Hence $z_\xi\in W$ and its specialization $z\in
W$. Thus $a:W\to B$ is surjective. This is the specialization argument of Rydh Lemma (3.2); it
does not assume flatness of $h$.

5. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-proper-closed-in-proper-fp] to $B\to C$: $C$ is affine, hence qcqs, and
step 3 proved properness. Write $B=\varprojlim_j B_j$ with $B_j\to C$ proper and finitely
presented, and all transitions and projections closed immersions. In particular the transitions
are affine. Every $B_j\to C$ is surjective, since the surjective $B\to C$ factors through it.
All $B_j$ are qcqs by [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence]. This approximation supplies
finite presentation without assuming it for the original blowup.

6. Over the affine-transition system $(B_j)$, apply [thm-pd-spreading-out](1) to the finitely
presented $W\to B$. Obtain $W_j\to B_j$ finitely presented. The schemes $Z\times_C B_j$ are also
finitely presented over $B_j$ by [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence]. Apply
[thm-pd-spreading-out](2) to descend the map $W\to Z\times_C B$ to $v_j:W_j\to Z\times_C B_j$
after increasing $j$. This retains the actual map to $X$ as part of the diagram, not merely the
abstract existence of a flat scheme.

7. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-descend-flat-finite-presentation] and [lem-weil-pbc-descend-surjective]
to $W_j\to B_j$, viewed over the system $(B_j)$ with target the identity scheme $B_j$. Their
limit map is $a$, flat by step 2 and surjective by step 4; the stage map is finitely presented,
and both source and target are qcqs. Choose a common later index where both properties hold.
Flatness and surjectivity persist under the intervening base changes by
[lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-base-change] and [lem-weil-pbc-surjective-base-change]. Thus we have
$W_j\to B_j\to C$ with the first arrow faithfully flat and finitely presented and the second
proper, surjective and finitely presented.

8. Fix that index and write $V=W_j$, $D=B_j$. The composite $V\to C$ is locally of finite
presentation by [lem-sbc-locally-finite-presentation-composition]; it is quasi-compact and
quasi-separated by [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence], since $V,D,C$ are qcqs. Thus $V\to
C$ and $D\to C$ are finitely presented. The descended $v_j$ gives a $C$-map $V\to X\times_Y C$
compatible with $V\to D\to C$.

9. By [lem-weil-pbc-integral-morphism-limit-of-finite], write $C=\varprojlim_i C_i$ over $Y$,
with $C_i\to Y$ finite and finitely presented and affine transitions. Each $C_i$ is affine and
qcqs, and $C_i\to Y$ is surjective because $C\to Y$ factors through it. Apply
[thm-pd-spreading-out] to the finitely presented $C$-schemes $V,D,X\times_Y C$ and the maps
$V\to D$, $V\to X\times_Y C$. Directedness gives a common stage; clause (3) ensures all diagram
equalities after increasing it. We obtain finitely presented $V_i,D_i$ over $C_i$ and maps
$a_i:V_i\to D_i$, $u_i:V_i\to X$ with $fu_i=(C_i\to Y)(D_i\to C_i)a_i$.

10. The map $a_i$ is locally of finite presentation by
[lem-a1-map-over-base-finite-presentation], since $V_i\to C_i$ is locally of finite presentation
and $D_i\to C_i$ is locally of finite type. It is quasi-compact and quasi-separated by
[lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence], since $V_i,D_i$ are qcqs, so is finitely presented.
Apply [lem-weil-pbc-descend-flat-finite-presentation] to $a_i$,
[lem-weil-pbc-descend-surjective] to $a_i$ and to $D_i\to C_i$, and
[lem-weil-pbc-eventually-proper] to $D_i\to C_i$. Their limit maps have precisely the needed
flatness, surjectivity and properness by step 7. Each stage arrow is locally of finite
presentation (hence locally of finite type for properness descent). All schemes are qcqs.
Increase $i$ to a common stage; flatness, surjectivity and properness persist under the further
base changes by [lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-base-change], [lem-weil-pbc-surjective-base-change]
and [lem-weil-pbc-proper-base-change].

11. Set $X_1=V_i$, $Y_1=D_i$, $u=u_i$, $a=a_i$, and $b=(C_i\to Y)\circ(D_i\to C_i)$. The finite
map $C_i\to Y$ is proper by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-proper], so $b$ is proper by
[lem-weil-pbc-proper-composition]. Its local finite presentation follows from
[lem-sbc-locally-finite-presentation-composition], and its quasi-compactness and
quasi-separatedness from [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence]; hence $b$ is finitely
presented. Both factors of $b$ are surjective, so $b$ is surjective. Step 10 gives the
faithfully flat finitely presented $a$, and step 9 gives $fu=ba$. For every $y\in Y$, first
choose $y_1$ over $y$ and then $x_1$ over $y_1$; hence $ba$ is a surjective refinement. If $Y$
is empty, take $X_1=Y_1=\varnothing$ and the same conclusions hold vacuously.
-/
theorem exists_flat_proper_refinement_of_isUniversallySubtrusive
    {X Y : Scheme.{u}} [IsAffine Y] (f : X ⟶ Y)
    [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f] [QuasiCompact f] [QuasiSeparated f]
    (hf : IsUniversallySubtrusive f) :
    ∃ (X₁ Y₁ : Scheme.{u}) (u : X₁ ⟶ X) (a : X₁ ⟶ Y₁) (b : Y₁ ⟶ Y),
      u ≫ f = a ≫ b ∧
      Flat a ∧ Surjective a ∧ LocallyOfFinitePresentation a ∧ QuasiCompact a ∧
      QuasiSeparated a ∧ IsProper b ∧ Surjective b ∧ LocallyOfFinitePresentation b ∧
      QuasiCompact b ∧ QuasiSeparated b ∧ Surjective (a ≫ b) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
